KNOWNBUG (front-end CRASH): std::optional<std::string> construction trips
the make_ptr_typecast precondition (pointer cast between unrelated
structs) inside typecheck_member_initializer while instantiating
libstdc++'s _Optional_payload/_Optional_base internals -- an invariant
violation, not a graceful error.  Found by dog-fooding CBMC's own sources
(util/simplify_expr.cpp, util/cmdline.cpp).  optional<int> works.  Flip
to CORE when the constructor chain converts.

UPDATE 2026-07-16 (2): the front-end CRASH is fixed -- two changes:
(1) [class.derived]/2 per-base recovery in typecheck_compound_bases (one
failing base specifier no longer drops its siblings during template
instantiation); (2) make_ptr_typecast degrades to a plain typecast
instead of tripping its precondition when the relation is not derivable
from (partially recovered) bases.  cmdline.cpp/simplify_expr.cpp
dog-fooding is unblocked.  REMAINING (why this stays KNOWNBUG):
optional<string>'s values are still nondet -- the root is that
__is_destructible_impl<basic_string>::type (decltype(__test<_Tp>(0))
SFINAE) fails to resolve ONLY inside optional's nested instantiation
context (works at user level: __is_destructible_safe<string,0,0>::value
verifies TRUE in main), so is_trivially_destructible degrades and
_Optional_base's bool args mismatch between the base specifier and the
constructor's member initializer.  Flip to CORE when the nested-context
trait SFINAE resolves.
